<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer post-content">

    <h2>What Is llms.txt?</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);"><code>llms.txt</code> is a plain-text file, written in simple Markdown, that sits at the root of your domain (like <code>robots.txt</code> or <code>sitemap.xml</code>) and gives AI assistants a short, structured summary of who you are and where your most important pages live. Unlike a sitemap, it isn't a crawl directive — it's a hand-written cheat sheet that helps an AI model describe your business accurately instead of guessing from whatever page it happens to land on.</p>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);">The format was proposed in 2024 by Jeremy Howard (Answer.AI) as a lightweight standard, and it's since been adopted by a growing list of developer tools, SaaS platforms, and documentation sites. It's still voluntary — no search engine or AI vendor requires it — but adoption is one-sided in cost: it takes minutes to add and there's no downside to having one.</p>

    <h2>llms.txt vs. robots.txt vs. sitemap.xml</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);">These three root files are often confused because they all live in the same place, but they do completely different jobs:</p>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
      <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:16px 0; font-size:0.95rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;">
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">File</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Purpose</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Audience</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>robots.txt</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Controls which pages crawlers are allowed to access</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Search engine &amp; AI crawlers</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>sitemap.xml</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Lists every indexable URL for discovery</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Search engine crawlers</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>llms.txt</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Summarizes who you are and what matters most, in plain language</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">AI assistants &amp; LLMs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);">None of the three replace the others — a well-optimized site typically has all three, each doing its own job.</p>

    <h2>Why It Matters for AI Search</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);">As more people ask ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity for recommendations instead of typing a Google query, being described correctly by those tools matters as much as ranking well in search results. See our guide to <a href="/blog/what-is-llm-seo-2026">LLM SEO</a> for the bigger picture, and <a href="/blog/how-to-get-cited-by-chatgpt-2026">how to get cited by ChatGPT</a> for what to do once your llms.txt is live.</p>

    <h2>What Makes a Good llms.txt File</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-dim);">A good file is short, accurate, and specific — not a copy-pasted homepage. Three things separate a useful llms.txt from a wasted one:</p>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>A one-line summary that could stand alone</strong> — if an AI only reads that one line, it should still describe your business correctly</li>
      <li><strong>Real page links, not just the homepage</strong> — point directly at the pages that answer specific questions (pricing, services, contact) rather than making the AI dig for them</li>
      <li><strong>Plain language, no marketing fluff</strong> — "family-owned plumbing company serving Round Rock since 2004" beats "your trusted partner for all things plumbing"</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Copying your meta description verbatim</strong> — llms.txt should add context a meta tag can't fit, not repeat it</li>
      <li><strong>Only listing the homepage</strong> — the whole point is pointing an AI straight at your most useful specific pages</li>
      <li><strong>Never updating it</strong> — an llms.txt describing services you dropped two years ago actively misleads the AI reading it</li>
      <li><strong>Placing it anywhere but the root</strong> — <code>yoursite.com/llms.txt</code> only, never in a subfolder</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Add It to Your Site</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li>Fill in the form above and click Download</li>
      <li>Rename the file to exactly <code>llms.txt</code> if your browser changed the name</li>
      <li>Upload it to your site's root directory (the same place <code>robots.txt</code> lives)</li>
      <li>Confirm it loads at <code>https://yoursite.com/llms.txt</code></li>
      <li>Update it whenever your key pages or offerings change</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Free llms.txt Generator FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Is llms.txt the same as robots.txt?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. robots.txt tells crawlers which pages they're allowed to access. llms.txt doesn't control access at all — it's a plain-language summary that helps AI assistants understand and describe your site once they've read it.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need to know how to code to add this?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Download the generated file and upload it to your site's root folder using your host's file manager or FTP client — the same place your <code>robots.txt</code> or <code>favicon.ico</code> already lives.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will this guarantee ChatGPT or Claude mentions my business?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No tool can guarantee that. llms.txt is still an emerging, voluntary convention that not every AI system reads yet — but it costs nothing to add and gives the ones that do read it accurate information instead of a guess.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How often should I update my llms.txt file?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Whenever your key pages, services, or offerings change meaningfully — there's no fixed schedule, treat it like your sitemap or meta description.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything you type is processed entirely in your browser with JavaScript — nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I have more than one page in the "Optional" section?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes — add as many lines as you need, one page per line in the same <code>Label | URL | Description</code> format. The Optional section is meant for secondary pages an AI can skip if it's short on context.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does every AI assistant actually read llms.txt today?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Not universally — adoption varies by vendor and is still evolving. Treat it the same way early adopters treated schema markup: not yet mandatory, but low-cost and forward-looking.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is this free tool a permanent part of Contomatix's site?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. It's one of several free SEO tools we're building — see the full, growing list at <a href="/tools">our free tools page</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

